# Hyper-markdown

> see [hypermarkdown.org](https://hypermarkdown.org)

**Hyper-markdown** (`.hmd`) is this project's most generic and most-used
knowledge format: plain Markdown extended with **wiki features**, above all
`[[wikilinks]]` between cards. It is the default home for cross-linked, reusable
explanations of a single idea. A `.hmd` file is a **card**, and cards live in a
**vault** — the single directory that bounds the graph. This project's vault is
[`doc/wiki/`](README.md). A card also reads fine as plain Markdown: the format
is Markdown plus a wiki graph, nothing more exotic, with no external toolchain
to satisfy.

## A card is a node in a graph

A card explains one concept and nothing else; its value is the links.
Cross-reference other cards liberally with `[[ ]]` so a whole model emerges
from small, connected cards rather than one long document. Keep it **DRY and
bidirectional**: never restate what another card owns — link to it — and make
each idea reachable from both directions.

## The vault

A vault is the namespace root: the one directory a `[[ ]]` link may resolve
inside, and the frame every reference is written against. A target that escapes
it is an error (`HMD003`), so the graph is closed by construction. **Everything
outside the vault** is reached by an ordinary relative Markdown link, never by
`[[ ]]`.

What makes a directory a vault is an `.hmd/` directory at its root — that
marker, and the vault state and configuration it holds, are owned by
[[card-type]]. The settings in [.hmd/config.toml](.hmd/config.toml) govern every
card beneath it and are optional; where a key is absent its default is assumed.

Binding a reference to a file is uniform: the reference plus `.hmd` is tried as
a file, and otherwise the reference is tried as a directory holding an
`index.hmd`. A trailing `.hmd` may be written and is stripped before matching.
No wikilink can name a `.md` file, so a document that is not `.hmd` is outside
the graph however it is filed.

## Writing a reference

A reference takes one of three forms, all of which may carry display text as
`[[target|display text]]` so the sentence still reads naturally.

- **Unqualified** — no leading path marker, and the form to reach for by
  default. It is either a *bare name* of one segment (`[[type-system]]`) or an
  *unqualified path* of two or more (`[[hql/knowledge]]`). Both are searched the
  same way; a path is not a special case but the remedy when a bare name is
  ambiguous.
- **Relative** — begins with `./` or `../`, and names a card by walking from the
  card that writes it.
- **Absolute** — begins with `/`, and names a card from the vault root.

Relative and absolute references bind directly. Unqualified ones are searched,
and the search is what the next section describes.

**Forward links are fine.** Linking a concept that has no card yet marks it as
worth writing later; an unresolved link is a warning (`HMD001`), not an error.

## How an unqualified reference resolves

Four phases, in order, first match wins:

1. **Named imports.** A bare name matching an `import` binding in the card's
   frontmatter resolves to that card immediately.
2. **The spine walk.** From the writing card's own directory upward to the
   vault root, probing each directory without recursing. This is pure path
   arithmetic and depends on nothing but the file tree.
3. **Imported search paths.** The directories named by wildcard imports, in
   declaration order, without recursing.
4. **The autodiscovery sweep.** Everything else in scope.

**This vault uses phases 2 and 4.** Its config pins the defaults:
autodiscovery on, mode `both`, so the sweep covers the whole vault rather than
only the writing card's subtree. No card declares an `import` or a `use`
directive, so phases 1 and 3 never fire.

## Autodiscovery is load-bearing here

The sweep is not a fallback this vault tolerates — it is how roughly a third of
the links resolve, and deliberately so. `[[card-type]]` written in `hql/` is the
ordinary case: the spine of a card in `hql/` never enters `hql/types/`, and the
link works because the sweep finds the card anywhere in the vault. That is the
feature being used, and it is what keeps a card free to move between subfolders
without its inbound links being rewritten.

What that costs is worth naming. Those links resolve **only** in the last phase.
Turning autodiscovery off — vault-wide with `autodiscovery = false` under
`[discovery]`, or for one card with `use: [no_autodiscovery]` in its frontmatter
— unresolves all of them at once. Adding a wildcard import can also silently
redirect a reference that had been resolving by sweep, since phase 3 runs first.
The rule that follows: **this vault depends on autodiscovery staying on**, and a
change to either setting is a change to the whole link graph. The config says so
where the setting is written.

## When to qualify a link

Qualify with an unqualified path when a bare name would match more than one
card. Two filenames occur twice in this vault: `knowledge.hmd` in `hql/` and
in `hql/extensions/`, and `hypermarkdown.hmd` at the root and in
`hql/extensions/`. A bare `[[knowledge]]` resolves on the spine to whichever is
nearer the writing card, but from a card whose spine reaches neither it falls to
the sweep, matches both, and is an ambiguity error (`HMD002`). The remedy is to
write `[[hql/knowledge]]`, not to memorise a tie-break.

## Frontmatter

A card's YAML header is **optional** — a card without one is well formed, and
most cards here have none. When present it may carry `tags` (hierarchical
labels, informational only), `use` (feature toggles, `no_`-prefixed to disable),
`import` (named bindings and wildcard search origins), and `nav` (publication
`order` and `visibility`).

## Writing a card

The authoring procedure — naming, structure, and register — is owned by the
`doc/hmd` documentation style, not restated here:
[doc/hmd](../../.grem/styles/doc/hmd/prompt.md). Running
`grem new --type doc --style hmd <source>` renders that style against a source
document for an agent to apply.

## Where it is used

Every card in the vault is hyper-markdown. It is one of the notations the
project's design layer under `doc/models/` may be written in, alongside more
formal specifications, and the format HQL itself reads as knowledge — see
[[hmd-integration]].
